The audience is first-time, never-licensed applicants for a Michigan operator's license, reading at a plain-English level, plus newcomers and parents of teen drivers who use the same handbook. This section covers passive and active railroad crossing controls, grade crossing safety, what to do when stuck on the tracks, Emergency Notification System signs, parking basics, hill and parallel parking, disability and van-accessible parking, and the no-parking rules. It teaches how to cross railroad tracks safely and react if stuck, how to park on hills and parallel park, who may use disability spaces, and where parking is forbidden. It assumes no prior driving knowledge and no prerequisites beyond literacy, so wording stays concrete, procedural and free of legal jargon throughout.
